@page "/progress"

<CatHead Title="Progress and spinners">
    Three ways to say &ldquo;working&rdquo;, picked by how much you know about what is coming.
    The fourth &mdash; the shape of what is coming, before it arrives &mdash; is a
    <a href="/skeleton">skeleton</a>.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-question-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Pick by what you know</span>
        A percentage is a <code>.progress</code> bar. No
        percentage, but a known shape — a table of rows, a card — is a
        <a href="/skeleton"><code>.skeleton</code></a>. Neither is a <code>.spinner</code>. Nothing at all yet, in a panel that will fill, is
        <a href="/empty-state"><code>.empty-state--pending</code></a>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.Busy" Title="Busy, on a control and on a surface" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>aria-busy="true"</code> is the one switch, and the paint follows what is announced. On a
        control — a menu item, a tab, a list row, a chip, a page link — its leading icon becomes the
        spinner and it takes no pointer, so the work cannot start twice; a <code>.btn</code> covers its
        label instead. On a surface — a card, a list, a table, a dialog, a drawer — the content stays
        in place, dimmed and out of reach, with a bar along the top edge. Under reduced motion both
        stand still.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.Progress" Title="Progress bar" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        Determinate: set the inline <code>width</code> on <code>.progress-bar</code>.
        Indeterminate: leave the width off and add <code>.progress--indeterminate</code> to the
        track. Put the number in text as well — a bar is a shape, and a reader deciding whether to
        wait needs the figure.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.Threshold" Title="Measuring towards a threshold" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.progress-bar--muted</code> is for a bar measuring progress <em>towards</em> a
        threshold rather than the completion of a task, where well below the line is neither a
        warning nor a failure. The base bar is <code>var(--brand)</code>, which reads as an alarm
        wherever the brand is a red.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.Severity" Title="Ranked bars" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.progress-bar--sev-1</code> … <code>--sev-5</code> take the ordered ramp behind the
        <a href="/badge">severity badges</a>, for a bar that measures a rank rather than a fraction.
        They are a fill and carry no label — where there is one, use <code>.badge-sev-N</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.Spinner" Title="Spinner">
    <p>
        Sized in <code>em</code>, so it matches whatever text or button it sits in without a second
        class. <code>.spinner--lg</code> is the standalone size for a panel that is still loading.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.Ring" Title="Progress ring" Demo="ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.progress-ring</code> is the determinate value where there is no width to fill.
        Write the value as a percentage in <code>--progress</code> and the same number in
        <code>aria-valuenow</code>; the element holds a label if you give it one.
        <code>--progress-ring-size</code> and <code>--progress-ring-width</code> size it, and
        <code>.progress-ring--lg</code> is the larger step and <code>.progress-ring--sm</code> the smaller, beside a line of text. <code>.spinner--sm</code> is three quarters of the text it sits in.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.RingRound" Title="Round ends, and a ring that glides" Demo="ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.progress-ring--round</code> rounds both ends of the arc, in every size. Every ring
        glides from one value to the next, as a <code>.progress-bar</code> does, and lands at once
        under reduced motion &mdash; so write each new value as it comes and leave the animating to
        the stylesheet. Blazor's own <code>--blazor-load-percentage</code> works as the value while
        WebAssembly starts.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Progress/Splash.html" Title="A splash while the app starts">
    <p>
        <code>.splash</code> covers the viewport in the theme's background while the app starts,
        clear of the notch. Put it inside the element the app renders into, so the first render
        replaces it. Blazor WebAssembly writes <code>--blazor-load-percentage</code> on the
        document while it downloads, so a ring fed from it fills as the app loads.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatSnippet Name="Interop/RouteProgress.txt" Title="While the next page loads">
    <p>
        <code>.progress--route</code> lays a bar along the top edge of the window, above the
        frame, for the moment between a click and the page it leads to. Write it once, in the
        layout, and show it with the <code>hidden</code> attribute: off by default, on when a
        navigation starts, off again when it has arrived. It fades in only after a moment, so a
        page that arrives at once shows nothing, and it stands still under reduced motion.
    </p>
    <p>
        With interactive rendering the layout hears both ends from <code>NavigationManager</code>,
        as here. A page that then loads its own data shows a <a href="/skeleton">skeleton</a>
        for it. A statically rendered app with enhanced navigation has no circuit to do this from:
        its own script takes <code>hidden</code> off when a link is followed and puts it back on
        Blazor&rsquo;s <code>enhancedload</code> event.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Progress.RunningJobs" Title="A list of running work" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Where these end up in practice: one row per job, with the shape that matches what is known —
        a percentage gets a determinate bar, a wait with no total gets the indeterminate one and a
        <code>.spinner</code>, and anything finished gets a <a href="/badge">badge</a> instead. Add
        <code>aria-busy="true"</code> to the row while it is running, and put the figure in text as
        well as in the bar.
    </p>
</CatExample>
